L346: ### 5.2 Performance Difference
L347: Moreover, we aim to quantify how the introduction of causal guidance affects the behavior and performance of the diffusion policy. To this end, we establish a theoretical guarantee characterizing the performance difference between the causality-guided policy and the base diffusion policy using the Performance Difference Lemma (PDL). The PDL expresses the difference in performance between two policies in terms of the expectation of the advantage function (cite98†Kakade and Langford, 2002 ).
L348: Specifically, for any initial state $s_{0}$, the performance gap between two policies $\pi$ and $\pi^{\prime}$ is given by
L349:  | $$V^{\pi}\left(s_{0}\right)-V^{\pi^{\prime}}\left(s_{0}\right)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d_{s_{0}}^{\pi}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot\mid s)}A^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)\right],$$  |
L350: where $V^{\pi}\left(s_{0}\right)=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{i}r\left(s_{i},a_{i}\right)\mid s_{0},\pi\right]$ is the discounted return (value function) of policy $\pi$, and $A^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)=Q^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)-V^{\pi^{\prime}}(s)$ is the advantage function under policy $\pi^{\prime}$ with transition probability $d=p\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},a_{t}\right)$. Thus, we construct our Theorem cite99†1 below.
L351: ###### Theorem 1.
L352: 
L353: Let $\pi$ be the base (unguided) diffusion policy and $\pi_{cg}$ be the causality-guided diffusion policy obtained by adding drift terms with interventions
L354: 
L355:  | $$\gamma_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))$$  |
L356: 
L357: Assume the advantage function $A^{\pi}(s,a)$ is finite, then the performance difference satisfies the bound
L358:  |  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  |
L359:  |  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)\big]}$  |
L360:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{cg}}\!\left[\int_{0}^{T}\Big\|\frac{\gamma_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))}{g(t)}\Big\|^{2}dt\right]}$  |
L361: which implies that
L362: 
L363:  | $$\left|J\left(\pi_{cg}\right)-J(\pi)\right|\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{\mathrm{KL}\left(\pi_{cg}\|\pi\right)}\right)$$  |
L364: ###### Proof of Theorem 1.
L365: 
L366: By the Performance Difference Lemma (PDL), we can first have
L367: 
L368:  | $$J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\Big[\,\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)}\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\big]\,\Big]$$  |
L369: 
L370: where $d^{\pi_{cg}}$ denotes the (discounted) state-visitation distribution under $\pi_{cg}$, which implies that
L371:  | $$\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]-\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]=\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}(a\mid s)-\pi(a\mid s))\big]$$  |
L372: 
L373: as $A^{\pi}(s,a)=Q^{\pi}(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)$ and $V^{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi}Q^{\pi}(s,a)$. Hence, this PDL can be represented by the factorized expectation:
L374:  | $$J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\Big]$$  |
L375: 
L376: For each state $s$, we can derive the upper bound of this expectation:
L377: 
L378:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\int|\,\pi_{cg}(a\mid s)-\pi(a\mid s)\,|\,da$$  |
L379: 
L380: The integral on the right is twice the total variation (TV) distance:
L381:  | $$\mathrm{TV}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s),\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)=\tfrac{1}{2}\int|\pi_{cg}-\pi|(a\mid s)\,da$$  |
L382: 
L383: Hence, we can have
L384: 
L385:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq 2\,\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\mathrm{TV}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s),\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)$$  |
L386: 
L387: By Pinsker’s inequality, for two probability measures $p$ and $q$, the total variation (TV) distance is bounded by the square root of half Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence as:
L388:  | $$\mathrm{TV}(p,q)\leq\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}(p\|q)}$$  |
L389: 
L390: Applying this to the conditional policies at each $s$, we can drive that:
L391: 
L392:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq 2\,\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}$$  |
L393: 
L394: which yields
L395:  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\,2\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\,\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}\Big]$  |
L396:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{2}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\,\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}\Big]$  |
L397: According to the Cauchy–Schwarz of the two nonnegative factors $u(s),v(s)$ under the state expectation:
L398: 
L399:  | $$\mathbb{E}_{s}\big[u(s)\,v(s)\big]\leq\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s}[u(s)^{2}]}\;\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s}[v(s)^{2}]}$$  |
L400: 
L401: Set $u(s)=\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|,v(s)=\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}$, we can have
L402:  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{2}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\;\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)\Big]}$  |
L403:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{2}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\;\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)\big]}$  |
L404: Under the diffusion formulation, the causility-guided policy $\pi_{cg}$ differs from the base policy $\pi$ through additional time-dependent drift terms in the reverse SDE.
L405: Girsanov’s theorem (or the likelihood ratio for continuous-time diffusions) gives, under suitable regularity and when the two processes share the same diffusion coefficient $g(t)$, that the KL divergence between the conditional action distributions (or between path measures conditioned on $s$) can be written as the expected time-integral of the squared drift difference normalized by the diffusion magnitude. Formally,
L406:  | $$\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{cg}}\!\left[\int_{0}^{T}\Big\|\frac{\gamma_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))}{g(t)}\Big\|^{2}dt\right]$$  |
L407: 
L408: where the expectation on the right is taken over the policy induced by $\pi_{cg}$.
L409: 
L410: Finally, we can conclude that
L411:  |  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  |
L412:  |  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{cg}}\!\left[\int_{0}^{T}\Big\|\frac{\gamma_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))}{g(t)}\Big\|^{2}dt\right]}$  |
L413: 
L414: ∎
L415: Theorem cite99†1 shows that the performance difference of CGDC is controlled by the KL divergence between the causality-guided policy and the original diffusion policy. Importantly, the KL divergence grows linearly with the magnitude of the added drift terms.
L416: Since the performance difference scales with the square root of the KL divergence, the resulting performance degradation increases only sub-linearly with respect to the drift magnitude, which means even if the causality guidance is not perfectly accurate, it will not lead to catastrophic performance collapse of the diffusion policy.
L417: ### 5.3 Gradient of Guidance
L418: Recently, several works have investigated diffusion models with guidance from a more rigorous theoretical perspective (cite100†Hu et al., 2023 ; cite101†Guo et al., 2024 ; cite102†Jiao et al., 2025 ). In particular, cite101†Guo et al. (2024) showed that gradient of well-designed guidance can serve as an estimator of the policy gradient.
L419: For example, for a policy is in linear Gaussian form $\mathcal{N}\left(g^{\top}a_{0},\Sigma\right)$ with diffusion action $a_{0}$ (denoised from $a_{t}$ at time $t$) with parameter $g$ and covariance $\Sigma$, a good guidance $\nabla_{a_{t}}\mathrm{G}\left(a_{t},t\right)$ can be: $\nabla_{a_{t}}\mathrm{G}\left(a_{t},t\right)\propto\nabla_{a_{t}}g^{\top}\mathbb{E}\left[a_{0}\mid a_{t}\right]$.
L420: Motivated by this theoretical insight, we establish the following proposition: the causal guidance term can serve as an estimator of the gradient of the causal value function, which enables direct optimization of causal rewards in a diffusion policy without explicitly modeling the value function.
L421: ###### Proposition 2.
L422: 
L423: Under Assumptions cite88†1 , let the gradient of causal value function be defined as $Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)\triangleq\mathbb{E}\left[r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right]$. Then the causal guidance term $g(a)\triangleq\nabla_{a}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$ used in the diffusion policy provides an unbiased estimator of the policy gradient as
L424:  | $$\nabla_{a}Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)\propto\nabla_{a}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$$  |
L425: ###### Proof.
L426: 
L427: Since $Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)\triangleq\mathbb{E}\left[r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right]$, we can have:
L428:  | $\displaystyle\nabla_{a_{t}}Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)$  | $\displaystyle=\nabla_{a_{t}}\int r_{t}p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)ds_{t+1}dr_{t}$  |
L429:  |  | $\displaystyle=\int r_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)ds_{t+1}dr_{t}$  |
L430:  |  | $\displaystyle=\int r_{t}p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)ds_{t+1}dr_{t}$  |
L431:  |  | $\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[r_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)\right]$  |
L432: Then, for one Monte Carlo sample of the reward $r_{t}$, we can have: $\nabla_{a_{t}}Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)\approx r_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$. With the normalization of $r_{t}$, we then have
L433: 
L434:  | $$\nabla_{a_{t}}Q_{\text{causal }}\left(s_{t},a_{t}\right)\propto\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p\left(s_{t+1},r_{t}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$$  |
L435: 
L436: ∎
L437: Proposition cite103†2 demonstrates that our causal guidance is not merely a heuristic for directing reward, but is aligned with stochastic gradient ascent on the expected reward. Moreover, it is agnostic to the specific policy formulation, ensuring scalability to different diffusion policy scenarios.
L438: Beyond reward optimization in diffusion policies, our causal guidance is general and can be seamlessly incorporated into score-matching–based diffusion approaches. Similar to Lemma 3 in cite101†Guo et al. (2024) , we extend the result to causal guidance in the score function $\mathbf{s}_{t}(x_{t})=\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{t}(x_{t})$.
